\transclude{CLT03}

\p{The way to think about a problem #{\Pi} is the question “does #{w} belong to #{\Pi}?”. E.g. let #{\Sigma = \lbrace 0, 1\rbrace }. The natural language problem of “does this string have a #{1}?” is equivalently thought of as the problem #{\Pi = \Sigma^* \setminus \lbrace 0, 00, 000, \ldots\rbrace }.}

\transclude{CLT04}

\transclude{CLT05}

\p{Given an algorithm #{\mathcal{A}} and a word #{w}, we define the \em{successor} of #{w} according to #{\mathcal{A}} as follows:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{(i)} Among all of the #{u_1, \ldots, u_n} find the one with the \em{smallest} index which appears as a subword of #{w}.}
\li{\strong{(ii)} Say #{u_i} is the result of the previous step. Find the first occurrence of #{u_i} in #{w}: #{w = t u_i v} for some words #{t, v}.}
\li{\strong{(iii)} The successor of #{w} is the word #{t u_i' v}.}
}

